<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Horn and East Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Damot</h1><p class="st-meta">1200 CE – 1316 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A powerful non-Christian kingdom of the southwestern Ethiopian highlands that rivalled Shewa and the Muslim sultanates in the 13th century.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Damot is known mainly from Ethiopian Christian traditions and hagiographies. In the 13th century it was a strong kingdom south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blue-nile">Abay</a> (Blue <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>), and tradition says its ruler, remembered under the title Motalami, forced Muslim and Christian neighbours in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-shewa">Shewa</a> to pay tribute.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Little is known of its government or society. Its people followed traditional religions, and saints' lives describe the missionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tekle-haymanot-saint">Tekle Haymanot</a>, founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/debre-libanos">Debre Libanos</a>, preaching there. Damot lay on routes that brought gold and slaves from the southwest to the highland and coastal markets.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Solomonic emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amda-seyon-i">Amda Seyon</a> conquered Damot around 1316 and made it a province of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a>. The name lived on as a province, and from the 16th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oromo-migrations">Oromo migrations</a> transformed the region.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-damot.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Damot" width="320" height="277"><figcaption>Image: LeGabrie · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Approximate_extension_of_the_Zagwe_Empire.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1200 CE – 1316 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Traditional religion</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Damot</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1258&amp;focus=kingdom-of-damot" aria-label="Kingdom of Damot on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5717 1827 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Damot lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6037 2083L6029 1964"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6029 1964h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6037 2083h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6037 2083h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1258&amp;focus=kingdom-of-damot">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

